	My suggestion would be to reconstruct the idea of spell 
use/books.  Instead of spell books, as they now are, you'd have spell 
scrolls (holding spells) and magical scrolls (as we now have) and your 
spell using character would have to copy spells from spell scrolls into a 
spell book.  One spell book for all the spells.  And it wouldn't fade. (I 
$#^#% hate that.)
	Wizards would start with a spell book, and Priests and Healers 
would have a percentage chance of starting with one.  Everyone else would 
have to chance across one.
	Then you'd end up with a class of items "books" and not spell 
books.  A spell book would be an item in that class.  Then other types of 
books couple be added ala AD&D Manual of Golems, Libram of Gainful 
Conjuration, etc.
	And when you ran across a spell scroll and couldn't I.D. it.  You 
could just read it and have the spell effect occur... then the scroll 
vanishes.

	I've thought about this because I like to play Wizards, being the 
only class I haven't been able to get very far.  And they are incredibly 
weak.  The only way I've been able to get a reasonably decent one it for 
him to have an 18 con and find gauntlets of power... just so I can carry 
around the damned spell books and other crap a non-fighting character 
needs to survive.

	BTW, Fireball is a wonderful spell. :)

